<pre class="metadata">
title: Compartments
stage: 1
contributors: Mark S. Miller, Caridy Patiño, Patrick Soquet, Kris Kowal, Jack Works, Guy Bedford
</pre>

<emu-intro id="sec-intro">
  <h1>Introduction</h1>
  <p>
    A Compartment owns a module-instance memo while reusing the global object of
    the realm in which it is constructed. It gives a host another module graph,
    not another mandatory global runtime context.
  </p>
  <p>
    This proposal is a small extension of the ECMA-262 module model. A source
    phase import obtains a source object. A Compartment selects an instance for
    that source object, and ordinary module loading, linking, and evaluation
    advance that instance. The proposal adds neither module descriptors, module
    tables, resolver or load-hook protocols, nor a required fresh global object.
  </p>
  <p>
    Normative references to the existing module lifecycle are to ECMA-262
    <span class="secnum">16.1.1.4</span>, <span class="secnum">16.1.1.5</span>,
    and the <code>Link</code> and <code>Evaluate</code> abstract methods of
    Module Records. The source-phase and deferred-import proposals own their
    syntax and their phase-specific behaviour.
  </p>
</emu-intro>

<emu-clause id="sec-compartment-source-keys">
  <h1>Compartment Source Keys</h1>
  <p>
    A <dfn>Compartment source key</dfn> is a source-phase module source object.
    It is opaque to this proposal. A value is a valid Compartment source key only
    if it has the source-record internal slot required by
    <code>%AbstractModuleSource%.prototype</code>. This test is an internal-slot
    brand test, not a prototype-chain or structural-property test.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two source keys are the same key only if they are the same object. In
    particular, two separately produced source objects remain distinct keys even
    when their source text is identical. Source-phase import supplies its usual
    stable source object for one base Module Record, so repeated acquisition of
    that source preserves this identity without requiring Compartments to inspect
    specifiers, source text, import attributes, or host metadata.
  </p>
  <p>
    A host-defined source representation may be a key only when it carries the
    same <code>%AbstractModuleSource%</code> brand. A module kind with no source
    representation continues to fail source-phase import as specified by the
    source-phase proposal; Compartments do not invent a replacement source value.
  </p>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-compartment-objects">
  <h1>Compartment Objects</h1>
  <p>
    Each Compartment instance has the following internal slots:
  </p>
  <emu-table id="table-compartment-internal-slots">
    <table>
      <tr><th>Internal Slot</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>[[Realm]]</code></td>
        <td>The current Realm Record when the Compartment is constructed.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>[[GlobalObject]]</code></td>
        <td>The global object of <code>[[Realm]]</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>[[ModuleInstances]]</code></td>
        <td>A Map from Compartment source keys to the Compartment's Module Records.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>[[ExportsNamespaces]]</code></td>
        <td>A Map from Compartment source keys to deferred Compartment exports namespaces.</td>
      </tr>
    </table>
  </emu-table>

  <emu-clause id="sec-compartment-constructor">
    <h1>The Compartment Constructor</h1>
    <p>
      The <code>Compartment</code> constructor takes no arguments. It creates a
      Compartment with fresh empty <code>[[ModuleInstances]]</code> and
      <code>[[ExportsNamespaces]]</code> Maps. It sets <code>[[Realm]]</code> to
      the current Realm Record and <code>[[GlobalObject]]</code> to that Realm
      Record's global object.
    </p>
    <p>
      Module code instantiated through this Compartment executes in
      <code>[[Realm]]</code> and observes <code>[[GlobalObject]]</code> as its
      global object. Construction does not create a Realm, a global object, or an
      evaluator. A later proposal may add a distinct-global evaluator layer; it
      cannot change the behaviour of this constructor.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-compartment-prototype-exports">
    <h1>Compartment.prototype.exports ( _source_ )</h1>
    <p>
      The <code>exports</code> method returns the deferred Compartment exports
      namespace for <var>source</var>. It performs its brand checks
      synchronously. It does not instantiate, load, link, or evaluate a module.
    </p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let <var>compartment</var> be the <emu-val>this</emu-val> value.
      1. If <var>compartment</var> does not have a <code>[[ModuleInstances]]</code> internal slot, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Perform ? RequireCompartmentSourceKey(<var>source</var>).
      1. Let <var>namespace</var> be ! GetCompartmentExportsNamespace(<var>compartment</var>, <var>source</var>).
      1. Return <var>namespace</var>.
    </emu-alg>
    <p>
      A deferred Compartment exports namespace is not an <code>import defer</code>
      namespace. Before its <code>[[Module]]</code> is set, it is an identity for
      the host linker and has no observable exported bindings. Once its module is
      linked, it provides that module's ordinary exported names and live bindings.
      It retains its object identity before and after linking.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-compartment-prototype-import">
    <h1>Compartment.prototype.import ( _source_ )</h1>
    <p>
      The <code>import</code> method obtains the Compartment's instance for
      <var>source</var>, loads its dependencies, links it, evaluates it, and
      returns a Promise for the Compartment exports namespace. It has the same
      asynchronous completion shape as dynamic <code>import()</code>.
    </p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let <var>compartment</var> be the <emu-val>this</emu-val> value.
      1. If <var>compartment</var> does not have a <code>[[ModuleInstances]]</code> internal slot, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Perform ? RequireCompartmentSourceKey(<var>source</var>).
      1. Let <var>namespace</var> be ! GetCompartmentExportsNamespace(<var>compartment</var>, <var>source</var>).
      1. Let <var>module</var> be ! GetOrCreateCompartmentModule(<var>compartment</var>, <var>source</var>).
      1. Let <var>promiseCapability</var> be ! NewPromiseCapability(%Promise%).
      1. Let <var>loadPromise</var> be <var>module</var>.LoadRequestedModules().
      1. When <var>loadPromise</var> is fulfilled, perform <var>module</var>.Link(). If it completes abruptly, reject <var>promiseCapability</var> with that completion value.
      1. If linking succeeds, let <var>evaluationPromise</var> be <var>module</var>.Evaluate().
      1. When <var>evaluationPromise</var> is fulfilled, resolve <var>promiseCapability</var> with <var>namespace</var>. When it is rejected, reject <var>promiseCapability</var> with the same reason.
      1. When <var>loadPromise</var> is rejected, reject <var>promiseCapability</var> with the same reason.
      1. Return <var>promiseCapability</var>.[[Promise]].
    </emu-alg>
    <p>
      <code>import</code> performs no string-based resolution, fetching, or source
      compilation. Passing a non-source key is therefore a synchronous API error.
      A failure while loading dependencies, linking, or evaluating rejects the
      returned Promise. In particular, an evaluation error is never reported as a
      separate Compartment-specific error channel.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-compartment-module-memoization">
    <h1>Module Memoization</h1>
    <p>
      GetOrCreateCompartmentModule(<var>compartment</var>, <var>source</var>)
      returns <var>compartment</var>.<code>[[ModuleInstances]]</code>[<var>source</var>]
      when that entry exists. Otherwise it creates one Module Record from
      <var>source</var> in <var>compartment</var>.<code>[[Realm]]</code>, stores it
      in that Map before loading its requested modules, obtains the deferred
      exports namespace for the same pair, and sets that namespace's
      <code>[[Module]]</code> to the new Module Record.
    </p>
    <p>
      Thus one Compartment has at most one module instance for one source key,
      including while the instance is loading or linking. Different Compartments
      have different Maps. They create different instances for the same source key
      and retain different exported-state and namespace identities, even if they
      share one Realm and global object.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-require-compartment-source-key" aoid="RequireCompartmentSourceKey">
    <h1>RequireCompartmentSourceKey ( _source_ )</h1>
    <emu-alg>
      1. If <var>source</var> is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. If <var>source</var> does not have a <code>[[ModuleSourceRecord]]</code> internal slot, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Return unused.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-get-compartment-exports-namespace" aoid="GetCompartmentExportsNamespace">
    <h1>GetCompartmentExportsNamespace ( _compartment_, _source_ )</h1>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let <var>namespace</var> be <var>compartment</var>.<code>[[ExportsNamespaces]]</code>[<var>source</var>].
      1. If <var>namespace</var> is not empty, return <var>namespace</var>.
      1. Let <var>namespace</var> be a new deferred Compartment exports namespace with <code>[[Compartment]]</code> <var>compartment</var>, <code>[[SourceKey]]</code> <var>source</var>, and <code>[[Module]]</code> empty.
      1. Set <var>compartment</var>.<code>[[ExportsNamespaces]]</code>[<var>source</var>] to <var>namespace</var>.
      1. Return <var>namespace</var>.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-get-or-create-compartment-module" aoid="GetOrCreateCompartmentModule">
    <h1>GetOrCreateCompartmentModule ( _compartment_, _source_ )</h1>
    <p>
      If <var>compartment</var>.<code>[[ModuleInstances]]</code>[<var>source</var>]
      exists, return it. Otherwise, create a Module Record for <var>source</var>
      in <var>compartment</var>.<code>[[Realm]]</code>, store it in
      <var>compartment</var>.<code>[[ModuleInstances]]</code> before loading any
      requested modules, obtain GetCompartmentExportsNamespace(<var>compartment</var>,
      <var>source</var>), and set its <code>[[Module]]</code> slot to the new
      Module Record. Return the new Module Record.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-compartment-cross-compartment-linking">
  <h1>Cross-Compartment Linking</h1>
  <p>
    Existing host module loading selects the Module Record for each ModuleRequest.
    When that selection targets a Compartment module, the host retains the target
    Compartment and source key while it processes the request. It must use the
    target Compartment's memoized Module Record, not create an instance in the
    importing Compartment. This proposal adds no JavaScript resolver callback,
    loader callback, module table, or descriptor return protocol.
  </p>
  <p>
    A host may obtain <code>target.exports(targetSource)</code> before it has
    created the target Module Record. That stable deferred namespace identifies
    the target of a cross-Compartment edge. When the target is later imported,
    GetOrCreateCompartmentModule installs the target Module Record behind the same
    namespace. An edge arranged this way must not cause a local instance of
    <code>targetSource</code> in the importing Compartment.
  </p>
  <p>
    The host makes the requesting Compartment, its <code>[[Realm]]</code>, and its
    <code>[[GlobalObject]]</code> available to its existing module-loading
    machinery. A loader can therefore select the target instance in the intended
    Compartment while using the target context. Multiple Compartments sharing one
    global do not imply multiple loader registrations.
  </p>
  <p>
    A graph containing such edges is linked and evaluated by the ordinary Module
    Record algorithms. The deferred namespace is installed before dependency
    traversal, so a cycle may traverse a Compartment boundary and return to its
    starting Compartment. It does not introduce a second linker, an eager copy of
    exports, or a Compartment-specific cycle rule.
  </p>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-compartment-tla-and-errors">
  <h1>Top-Level Await and Errors</h1>
  <p>
    A Compartment module uses the ordinary asynchronous evaluation contract of
    Module Record <code>Evaluate</code>. If its transitive graph contains
    top-level await, the Promise returned by <code>compartment.import(source)</code>
    remains pending until the ordinary evaluation dependency graph completes. A
    cross-Compartment edge is an edge in that same graph; it neither bypasses nor
    restarts top-level-await evaluation.
  </p>
  <p>
    If linking fails, <code>import</code> rejects with the linking error. If a
    module evaluation fails, every dependent evaluation follows ECMA-262 cyclic
    module error propagation and <code>import</code> rejects with that evaluation
    error. Re-importing the same source in the same Compartment observes the
    memoized module's ordinary completed or errored state.
  </p>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-compartment-module-harmony-intersection">
  <h1>Intersection with Module Phases</h1>
  <emu-clause id="sec-compartment-source-phase-imports">
    <h1>Source-Phase Imports</h1>
    <p>
      Static <code>import source</code> retains its grammar, request phase, host
      loading, and source-representation errors. Its result may be passed to
      <code>exports</code> or <code>import</code> as a Compartment source key.
      Compartments do not reify a source from a specifier and do not alter source
      phase cache identity.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>
  <emu-clause id="sec-compartment-source-phase-import-expression">
    <h1>Source-Phase Import Expressions</h1>
    <p>
      <code>import.source(specifier)</code> retains its Promise result and source
      phase. After that Promise fulfills with a source object, a caller may pass
      the object to a Compartment. A rejection from <code>import.source</code> is
      its own source-phase failure and is not converted into a Compartment import
      result.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>
  <emu-clause id="sec-compartment-import-defer">
    <h1>Import Defer</h1>
    <p>
      <code>import defer</code> retains its modifier syntax and deferred namespace
      exotic-object semantics. A deferred Compartment exports namespace is a
      different object with a different purpose: it reserves the identity of a
      Compartment target for linking. Compartments do not make property access
      start deferred evaluation and do not turn <code>import defer</code> into an
      attribute or a loader protocol.
    </p>
    <p>
      The import-defer proposal's treatment of top-level-await subgraphs remains
      unchanged. If an import-defer request reaches a module selected in a
      Compartment, ordinary phase processing and the selected module's evaluation
      graph determine the result.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-annex id="sec-compartment-node-viability" informative>
  <h1>Node.js Viability Status</h1>
  <p>
    This annex records the current minimum against the project charter. It is not
    a Node.js API specification.
  </p>
  <emu-table id="table-node-viability-status">
    <table>
      <tr><th>Charter item</th><th>Status</th><th>Disposition</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Share the caller's global</td><td>Met</td><td><code>new Compartment()</code> records the current Realm and its global object.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Context-aware resolution</td><td>Met</td><td>The existing host loader receives the requesting Compartment, Realm, and global while selecting a target.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Phase information on requests</td><td>Met</td><td>Compartments consume existing ModuleRequest phase information and define no replacement.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One loader registration per context</td><td>Met</td><td>Compartments keep instance Maps. They do not register a loader, including when several share one global.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Composable with <code>module.registerHooks()</code></td><td>Met at this boundary</td><td>No Compartment hook intercepts or replaces the host hook layer. A Node integration test remains implementation work.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Error separation</td><td>Shortfall</td><td>The source-key API has only synchronous brand errors and asynchronous load, link, and evaluation failures. A Node loader-registration API is needed to specify synchronous infrastructure errors.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>TLA and synchronous evaluation paths</td><td>Shortfall</td><td><code>Compartment.prototype.import</code> is deliberately asynchronous. No synchronous evaluation entry point is included.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Loader-level callback lifetime</td><td>Met at this boundary</td><td>The minimum surface accepts no callbacks and retains only source-key, module, and namespace identities.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Meaningful base-loader defaults</td><td>Deferred</td><td>The minimum surface defines no overridable loader class. A future loader API must specify delegation defaults with that API.</td></tr>
    </table>
  </emu-table>
  <p>
    Maintainer decision requested: should a follow-on add a host-only synchronous
    evaluation operation, rejected for graphs with top-level await, or should
    Node expose that operation outside the Compartment JavaScript API? That is the
    only unresolved choice in this increment.
  </p>
</emu-annex>

<emu-annex id="sec-compartment-test262-frontier" informative>
  <h1>test262 Families Unblocked</h1>
  <p>
    This increment unblocks exactly these proposed staging families under
    <code>test/staging/Compartments/</code>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>constructor/shared-realm-global</code></li>
    <li><code>source-key/brand-and-identity</code></li>
    <li><code>instance-memoization/same-compartment</code></li>
    <li><code>instance-memoization/separate-compartments</code></li>
    <li><code>import/async-namespace-and-errors</code></li>
    <li><code>tla/dependency-and-error-propagation</code></li>
    <li><code>cross-compartment/deferred-exports-identity</code></li>
    <li><code>cross-compartment/cyclic-linking</code></li>
    <li><code>intersection/source-phase-static-and-expression</code></li>
    <li><code>intersection/import-defer-and-tla</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Observable consequences include stable equality of repeated
    <code>exports(source)</code> calls, equality between that object and the
    fulfilled value of <code>import(source)</code>, one module instance per
    Compartment and source key, distinct instances in distinct Compartments, and
    ordinary Promise timing and rejection identity for top-level await and
    evaluation failures.
  </p>
</emu-annex>
